.TH SU 1 "July 18, 1988"
.UC
.SH NAME
su \- substitute user id temporarily
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B su
[ \-f ] [ \- ] [ username [ args ... ] ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Su
demands the password of the specified
.I username,
and if it is given,
changes to that 
.I username
and invokes the Shell
.IR sh (1)
or
.IR csh (1)
without changing the current directory.
The user environment
is unchanged except for HOME and SHELL,
which are taken from the password file
for the user being substituted
(see
.IR environ (7)).
The new user ID stays in force until the Shell exits.
.PP
If no 
.I username
is specified, ``root'' is assumed.  You may 
.I su 
to ``root'' only if you are a member of the ``wheel'' group, 
even if no root password is set.
To 
.I su
to ``root'', you must supply the root password.
To remind the super-user of his responsibilities,
the Shell substitutes `#' for its usual prompt.
.PP
.I args
arguments are passed to the shell of
.I username
which 
.I su 
invokes. This allows
.I su
to execute commands as another user.
.PP
The
.B \-f
option prevents
.IR csh (1)
from executing the .cshrc file; thus making
.I su
start up faster.
.PP
The
.B \-
option simulates a full login.
.PP
An alternative implementation of \fIsu\fR is in \fB/bin/su.wheel\fR.
This version, which is shipped without the setuid-root bit turned on,
allows users in the ``wheel'' group (group 0) to
.I su
to ``root'' using
either their own password or the root password.
This version offers more convenience, but if your site is concerned about
the increased potential for security problems,
you should continue to use the standard version of \fIsu\fR.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sh(1), csh(1)
